<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Assumption of Mary</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dormition of the Mother of God</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Assumption of Mary is the Catholic dogma that Mary, at the end of her earthly life, was taken body and soul into heaven. Pope Pius XII defined it on 1 November 1950 in the apostolic constitution Munificentissimus Deus, leaving open whether she died first. Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> holds the equivalent belief as the Dormition, or Falling Asleep, of the Mother of God, and the feast is kept on 15 August. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> is silent on the end of her life; accounts appear in apocryphal Dormition narratives that scholars date from the fourth century or earlier, and the Emperor Maurice established the feast in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 600.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/assumption-of-mary.jpg" alt="Assumption of Mary" width="330" height="607" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Titian · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tizian_041.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
